   "title": "How lipsync works",
   "properties": {
    "ue_properties": {
     "widget_ue_connectable": {},
     "version": "7.8",
     "input_ue_unconnectable": {}
    }
   },
   "widgets_values": [
    "LIPSYNC\n\nH3 is a JOINT audio-video model. NativeAudioLock:\n  1. encodes real audio into the AUDIO stream of the AV latent\n     (auto-resamples to the VAE's 32kHz - source is usually 48k)\n  2. sets noise_mask = ones(video) / zeros(audio)\n     so ONLY video denoises\n  3. the video branch cross-attends to that fixed audio\n\nThat is what shapes the mouth.\n\nFeed it the VOCALS track, not the full mix - cleaner signal.\n\nThe ORIGINAL audio goes separately into the save node at the far right. Two different audio paths, do not confuse them."
   ],
   "color": "#432",
   "bgcolor": "#653"
  },
  {
   "id": 102,
   "type": "Note",
   "pos": [
    780,
    630
   ],
   "size": [
    300,
    465
   ],
   "flags": {},
   "order": 10,
   "mode": 0,
   "inputs": [],
   "outputs": [],
   "title": "DENOISE - read before changing",
   "properties": {
    "ue_properties": {
     "widget_ue_connectable": {},
     "version": "7.8",
     "input_ue_unconnectable": {}
    }
   },
   "widgets_values": [
    "DENOISE - THE CRITICAL DIAL\n\nDO NOT carry values over from SDXL-family models. H3 is FLOW MATCHING with a large sigma shift, so the same number means something very different.\n\nsigma = shift*t / (1 + (shift-1)*t)\n\nAt the default shift of 12:\n  denoise 0.02 -> effective 0.197\n  denoise 0.05 -> effective 0.387\n  denoise 0.15 -> effective ~0.66\n  denoise 0.25 -> effective 0.800\n\nThis graph ships denoise 0.45 as a BASE, because it is not applied flat. Node \"3b. Per-frame denoise\" scales it DOWN per frame by face size: small faces keep close to the full 0.45 and get synthesised, large faces drop to ~0.35x of it and keep the detail they already have.\n\nBase and scaling are tuned TOGETHER. If you bypass the per-frame node, drop the base a long way (0.15-0.25) or every large face gets rewritten.\n\nToo low: nothing changes.\nToo high: the head starts drifting relative to the body - a content problem no mask can hide.\n\nNOTE steps and denoise are INDEPENDENT. BasicScheduler builds a (steps/denoise)-long full-range schedule and keeps the lowest steps+1 sigmas, so 4 steps with a turbo LoRA is both fast and gentle.\n\nDo NOT use SplitSigmas for this - on a 4-step schedule at shift 12 even the last split point is already sigma 0.800."
   ],
   "color": "#432",
   "bgcolor": "#653"
  },
  {
